%I A267895 #28 Nov 16 2026 17:39:52
%S A267895 3,5,6,7,9,10,11,12,13,14,17,18,19,20,22,23,24,25,26,28,29,31,34,36,
%T A267895 37,38,40,41,43,44,46,47,48,49,50,52,53,56,58,59,61,62,67,68,71,72,73,
%U A267895 74,76,79,80,81,82,83,86,88,89,92,94,96,97,98,100,101,103,104,106,107,109
%N A267895 Numbers whose number of odd divisors is prime.
%C A267895 All odd primes are in the sequence.
%C A267895 From _Hartmut F. W. Hoft_, Nov 09 2026: (Start)
%C A267895 Every term k in the sequence has the form k = 2^m * p^s where m >= 0, p is an odd prime, and s+1 is prime.
%C A267895 The number of 2-dense sublists of divisors of a term k in this sequence is either 1 or s+1. This is the same number as the parts of the symmetric representation of sigma of k, SRS(k), - see the link in A384149. Therefore, SRS(k) has 1 part or s+1 parts. In either case the width pattern of SRS(k) has length 2*(s+1) - 1. For some values of prime s+1 these lengths again are primes (see A005382). (End)
%H A267895 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A267895/b267895.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A267895 The divisors of 36 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18, 36. The odd divisors of 36 are 1, 3, 9. There are 3 odd divisors of 36 and 3 is prime, so 36 is in the sequence.
%e A267895 From _Hartmut F. W. Hoft_, Nov 09 2026: (Start)
%e A267895 For a(12) = 18 = 2 * 3^2, SRS(a(12)) has width pattern 1 2 1 2 1 of prime length 5.
%e A267895 For a(93) = 162 = 2 * 3^4, SRS(a(93)) has width pattern 1 2 1 2 1 2 1 2 1 of length 9.
%e A267895 For a(1620) = 5184 = 2^6 * 3^4, SRS(a(1620)) has the unimodal width pattern 1 2 3 4 5 4 3 2 1 of length 9 (see T(5, 1) in A401361). (End)
%t A267895 Select[Range[100], PrimeQ[DivisorSigma[0, #/2^IntegerExponent[#, 2]]] &] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Dec 03 2020 *)
%o A267895 (PARI) isok(n) = isprime(sumdiv(n, d, (d%2))); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Apr 04 2016
